      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1147,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond spend more time near the oxygen atom. This gives the oxygen end of the molecule a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water molecules to stick together (a property called **cohesion**) and to stick to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it can exist naturally in all three states of matter at the temperatures found on Earth:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we are most familiar with. It is the only common substance that exists naturally in liquid form.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air.\n\n### 3. A Universal Solvent\nWater is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is crucial for life because it allows nutrients to move through plants and animals and helps transport waste out of the body.\n\n### 4. Abundance\nWater is the most abundant molecule on the surface of the Earth. It covers about 71% of the planet and makes up about 60% of the human body.\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It acts as a:\n*   **Chemical Reactant:** It is necessary for photosynthesis and cellular respiration.\n*   **Transport Medium:** It moves nutrients and oxygen to cells and carries away waste.\n*   **Temperature Regulator:** Water has a high \"specific heat,\" meaning it resists changes in temperature. This helps stabilize the Earth's climate and keeps the human body from overheating."
